  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nAfter weakening slightly to start the weak, global ensembles\nsupport the subtropical high restrengthening during the second\nhalf of the week, back up to a 600dam high, but also drifting a\nlittle further north toward/into UT. H5 heights over the local \narea are forecast to climb back up to a peak of around 596-597dam \nby Friday. Coolest temperatures over the next week are forecast\nfor Wednesday, but temperatures will still be above normal with \nlower desert highs in the 106-112 degree range. Despite the\nrestrengthening of the high, high temperatures are not forecast to\nsee a significant increase again, roughly 1 degree each day \nthrough Saturday, likely due to the the increased moisture.\nHowever, the forecast calls for just enough warming to once again\nincrease the coverage of Major HeatRisk. Morning low temperatures\nwill likely remain quite warm - barring any cooling from storms -\nmostly in the 80s to right around 90 degrees.\n\nWith the small movements of the subtropical high midweek and shift\nnorth late week, there will be a better fetch of easterly winds \nacross southern New Mexico, southern Arizona, and northern Mexico.\nAbove normal moisture is also shown to stick around through the\nend of the week and next weekend, with 60s dew points. This \npattern will at least support daily robust thunderstorms\ndeveloping over the AZ high terrain, but there is still\nuncertainty whether storms can fire off or survive into lower\ndesert areas like Phoenix and points further west. Still, this\nlooks like the best chance of the summer so fat for thunderstorms\nin portions of southwest AZ and southeast CA, who have been \nlacking for rain so far this summer. Latest NBM/WPC forecast \ncontinues to show 10-30% PoPs across much of south-central \nArizona by Thursday and continuing through the end of the week. A\nperiod of stronger mid-level easterlies and perhaps and easterly \nwave/IT will certainly improve the odds of storm chances across \nthe area Strong to severe wind gusts will be possible with most \nstorms and an increase in mixing ratios up to 11-12 g/kg late week\nwill support very heavy rainfall.",
